#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Example: Constrained optimization {#example-constrained-optimization}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The prior max-cut example is a common quadratic unconstrained binary "
"optimization problem. Q-CTRL's Optimization Solver can also solve "
"constrained optimization problems by passing hard constraints directly to "
"the Solver through the `constraint` input, instead of encoding them as "
"penalty terms in the objective function. The Solver currently supports "
"Hamming-weight-1 constraints: each constraint specifies a group of variables"
" where exactly one variable must equal 1 and the rest must equal 0.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following example demonstrates how to construct a cost function and a set of hard constraints for a constrained optimization problem, [graph partitioning](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_partition), by assigning every node in a graph to exactly one of several groups while minimizing the total weight of edges whose endpoints land in the same group.\n"
"In addition to the `qiskit-ibm-catalog` and `qiskit` packages, you will also use the following packages to run this example: `numpy`, `networkx`, and `sympy`. You can install these packages by uncommenting the following cell if you are running this example in a notebook using the IPython kernel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Define the problem {#1-define-the-problem-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Define a random graph partitioning problem by generating a graph with "
"randomly weighted nodes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/guides/q-ctrl-optimization-"
"solver/extracted-outputs/c2ce65e3-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A standard optimization model for weighted graph partitioning can be "
"formulated as follows. Split the nodes of the graph into three groups $g "
"\\in \\{0, 1, 2\\}$, and let $n_{i,g} = 1$ if node $i$ is assigned to group "
"$g$, and $n_{i,g} = 0$ otherwise. The goal is to minimize the total weight "
"of edges whose endpoints are assigned to the same group, where the weight of"
" an edge $(i,j)$ is the combined weight of its two endpoints, $\\omega_{i,j}"
" = \\omega_i + \\omega_j$:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\\textbf{Minimize}\\qquad y = \\sum_{(i,j)\\in E} \\omega_{i,j} \\sum_{g} "
"n_{i,g}\\, n_{j,g}$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every node must be assigned to exactly one of the three groups. This is a "
"Hamming-weight-1 constraint: for every node $i$, exactly one of $n_{i,0}, "
"n_{i,1}, n_{i,2}$ must equal 1, and the rest must equal 0:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$n_{i,0} + n_{i,1} + n_{i,2} = 1 \\texttt{ for all } i \\in V$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rather than encoding this requirement as a penalty term in the cost "
"function, pass it directly to the Solver as a hard constraint using the "
"`constraint` input.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Partially constrained problems\">\n"
"You do not need to add every variable to `constraint`. Any variable left out of the dictionary remains unconstrained, so you can mix hard-constrained groups of variables with free variables in the same problem.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Run the problem {#2-run-the-problem-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Get the result {#3-get-the-result}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Retrieve the solution and analyze the results. The solution cost represents "
"the total weight of edges whose endpoints ended up in the same group, so a "
"lower cost indicates a better partitioning of the graph.\n"
msgstr ""

